# Rust Coding Conventions

## Naming Rules

| Target | Format | Example |
|------|------|-----|
| Crate names | `snake_case` | `engine_core` |
| Modules/files | `snake_case` | `scene_manager.rs` |
| Structs/enums/traits | `PascalCase` | `SceneManager`, `CharaType` |
| Functions/methods/variables | `snake_case` | `load_scene()`, `chara_id` |
| Constants | `SCREAMING_SNAKE_CASE` | `MAX_ENTITIES` |
| Type parameters | Single capital letter or semantic name | `T`, `S: System` |
| Lifetimes | Short lowercase names | `'a`, `'ctx` |

## Error Handling

- Library crates: define typed errors with `thiserror`.
- Application crates: use `anyhow` for concise error propagation.
- `unwrap()` / `expect()` are forbidden in production code (only allowed for explicit panics in tests/initialization).
- Consolidate error types in `error.rs` per module.

```rust
// ✅ Good
use thiserror::Error;

#[derive(Debug, Error)]
pub enum AssetError {
    #[error("asset not found: {path}")]
    NotFound { path: String },
    #[error("failed to decode: {0}")]
    Decode(#[from] std::io::Error),
}

// ❌ Bad
fn load() {
    let data = std::fs::read("file").unwrap(); // panic risk
}
```

## `unsafe` Rules

- Restrict `unsafe` to FFI boundaries only (Live2D SDK, Filament API).
- Every `unsafe` block must have a `// SAFETY:` comment justifying soundness.
- Isolate `unsafe` wrappers in dedicated modules (`ffi/`) and expose safe public APIs.

```rust
// SAFETY: Filament Engine::create is null-checked.
// The engine lifetime is tied to the application lifetime.
unsafe {
    let engine = filament_sys::Engine_create();
    assert!(!engine.is_null());
}
```

## Linting and Formatting

- Always pass `cargo fmt -- --check`.
- Treat warnings as errors via `cargo clippy -- -D warnings`.
- Recommended Clippy lint groups:

```toml
# Cargo.toml (workspace)
[workspace.lints.clippy]
pedantic = { level = "warn", priority = -1 }
nursery = { level = "warn", priority = -1 }
unwrap_used = "deny"
expect_used = "warn"
```

## Documentation

- Add `///` doc comments to all public APIs (`pub`).
- Add module-level overviews using `//!`.
- Explain intent for complex logic with `//` comments.
- Include an `# Examples` section in doc comments and keep it verifiable via `cargo test --doc`.

## Module Structure

- Split modules at around 400 lines per file.
- Keep `mod.rs` for re-exports only; no business logic.
- Minimize visibility (`pub(crate)` -> `pub(super)` -> `pub`).

## Tests

- Unit tests: `#[cfg(test)] mod tests` in the same file.
- Integration tests: under `tests/` directory.
- Test function naming: `test_<target>_<condition>_<expected_result>`.

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;

    #[test]
    fn test_asset_loader_missing_file_returns_not_found() {
        let result = load_asset("nonexistent.png");
        assert!(matches!(result, Err(AssetError::NotFound { .. })));
    }
}
```

## Leverage the Type System

- Represent state transitions with enums + typestate patterns.
- Use `newtype` to prevent misuse of primitive types.
- Apply appropriate trait bounds to generics.

```rust
// ✅ Clarify domain types with newtype
pub struct CharaId(pub String);
pub struct SoundId(pub String);
```

## Concurrency

- Prefer `mpsc::channel` (message passing) over `Arc<Mutex<T>>` for cross-thread sharing.
- Use ECS framework mechanisms for inter-system data transfer.
- Design data with `Send + Sync` boundaries in mind.
